    m({CSMA BLT LLC})                        = 0.3200
    m({FIRSTKEY HOMES})                      = 0.1200
    m({CSMA BLT LLC,FIRSTKEY HOMES})         = 0.0800

    Unresolved conflict: 48.00% of belief mass. The deed
    record (grantee CSMA BLT LLC, doc 1717247010) and the current
    assessment roll (FIRSTKEY HOMES) disagree. Field semantics
    (ruling R1, not evidence): the assessor mailing-name field can
    reflect a property manager rather than the title holder --
    this conflict reads as REQUIRES VERIFICATION, not as proof of
    a competing claim.

  Proposition: sslbda_disposition
    m(emptyset)                              = 0.4000 [CONFLICT]
    m({conveyed})                            = 0.1000
    m({not_conveyed})                        = 0.4000
    m({conveyed,not_conveyed})               = 0.1000

    Unresolved conflict: 40.00% of belief mass --
    contradictory records requiring curative work:
    the SSLBDA CRM row (feature 258: disposition 'Sold',
    date 2017-01-01) conflicts with recorded deed doc
    1717247010 (2017-06-16, grantor RICHARD THORTON,
    grantee CSMA BLT LLC -- SSLBDA is not a party).
    Resolution requires correcting the CRM record or
    locating a recorded SSLBDA conveyance; none exists
    in the queried datasets.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
SECTION 6: SHARED-UPSTREAM DEMONSTRATION
[ILLUSTRATION -- real linked pair: ccao_parcel_sales, cc_assessor]
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  current_owner, linked (provenance-aware):  1 evidence class
  current_owner, unlinked:                   2 evidence classes
  Fused belief byte-identical:               YES

  FOIL (count-based confidence): a naive pipeline would report
  'ownership confirmed by 2 sources' while both derive from
  county systems -- and here they DISAGREE, which a count
  presents as a 50/50 tie. Provenance-aware fusion keeps the
  frozen per-source-type confidences, makes the conflict
  explicit (48.00% m(emptyset)), and linking cannot
  understate evidence -- only prevent overstatement (idempotence).

